WEBVTT
1
00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.660
Retrieval Augmented Generation,
or RAG, has become a key

2
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.368
method for getting LLMs
to answer questions over a user's

3
00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:13.780
own data. But to actually build and
productionize a high-quality RAG

4
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.000
system, it costs a lot to have
effective retrieval techniques, to

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.557
give the LLM highly
relevant context to

6
00:00:20.557 --> 00:00:25.161
generate his answer, and also
to have an effective evaluation framework

7
00:00:25.161 --> 00:00:27.462
to help you
efficiently iterate and

8
00:00:27.462 --> 00:00:31.362
improve your RAG system, both during
initial development and

9
00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:34.879
during post-deployment maintenance. This
course covers

10
00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:38.587
two advanced retrieval
methods, sentence window retrieval

11
00:00:38.587 --> 00:00:42.232
and auto-merging retrieval, that deliver
a significantly

12
00:00:42.232 --> 00:00:45.301
better context of the
LLM than simpler methods.

13
00:00:45.301 --> 00:00:47.858
It also covers how
to evaluate your LLM

14
00:00:47.858 --> 00:00:52.717
question-answering system with three evaluation
metrics, context relevance,

15
00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:55.786
groundedness, and answer
relevance. I'm excited

16
00:00:55.786 --> 00:00:59.686
to introduce Jerry Liu,
co-founder and CEO of LlamaIndex, and

17
00:00:59.686 --> 00:01:03.650
Anupam Datta, co-founder and Chief Scientist
of TruEra. For a

18
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:08.126
long time, I've enjoyed following Jerry
and LLamaIndex on social media

19
00:01:08.126 --> 00:01:10.172
and getting tips
on evolving RAG

20
00:01:10.172 --> 00:01:14.903
practices. So I'm looking forward to
him teaching this body of knowledge

21
00:01:14.903 --> 00:01:18.548
more systematically here. And Anupam has
been a professor

22
00:01:18.548 --> 00:01:20.977
at CMU and has
done research for over

23
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:23.407
a decade on trustworthy
AI and how to

24
00:01:23.407 --> 00:01:26.860
monitor, evaluate, and optimize
AI app effectiveness.

25
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.440
Thanks, Andrew. It's
great to be here.

26
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.280
Great to be with you, Andrew.

27
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.240
Sentence window retrieval
gives an LLM better context by

28
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.140
retrieving not just
the most relevant sentence,

29
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.720
but the window of
sentences that occur before

30
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.540
and after it in the document. Auto-merging
retrieval organizes the document

31
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.060
into a tree-like structure
where each parent node's text is

32
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.180
divided among its child nodes. When meta child
nodes are identified as relevant

33
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.460
to a user's question, then the
entire text of the parent node is

34
00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.620
provided as context for the
LLM. I know this sounds

35
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.560
like a lot of steps, but don't worry, we'll
go over it in detail

36
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.890
on code later. But the main takeaway is that
this provides a way to dynamically

37
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.260
retrieve more coherent chunks of text
than simpler methods.

38
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.450
To evaluate RAG-based LLM apps, the
RAG triad, a

39
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.420
triad of metrics for the three
main steps of a RAG's execution,

40
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:21.920
is quite effective. For example,
we'll cover in detail how to compute

41
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.800
context relevance, which measures how
relevant the retrieved chunks

42
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.940
of text are to
the user's question. This helps you identify

43
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.270
and debug possible issues with how
your system is

44
00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.700
retrieving context for
the LLM in the QA system.

45
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.180
But that's only part of the
overall QA system. We'll also cover additional evaluation

46
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.660
metrics such as groundedness
and answer relevance

47
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.560
that let you
systematically analyze what parts of your

48
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.530
system are or
are not yet working well so

49
00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.720
that you can go in in
a targeted way to improve whatever

50
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:05.150
part needs the most work. If
you're familiar with the concept of error

51
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:09.420
analysis and machine learning, this has
similarities. And I've found that

52
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.320
taking this sort of systematic
approach helps you be

53
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.360
much more efficient in
building a reliable QA

54
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:17.880
system.

55
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.124
The goal of this course
is to help you build production-ready,

56
00:03:22.124 --> 00:03:26.506
write-based LLM apps. And important
parts of getting production

57
00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:29.244
ready is to iterate
in a systematic way

58
00:03:29.244 --> 00:03:33.215
on the system. In the
later half of this course, you gain

59
00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:36.638
hands-on practice iterating
using these retrieval

60
00:03:36.638 --> 00:03:39.719
methods and evaluation
methods. And you also

61
00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:43.074
see how to use
systematic experiment tracking to

62
00:03:43.074 --> 00:03:46.429
establish a baseline and
then quickly improve on

63
00:03:46.429 --> 00:03:46.840
that.

64
00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.880
We'll also share some
suggestions for tuning

65
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.060
these two retrieval
methods based on

66
00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.020
our experience assisting
partners who are

67
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:53.920
building RAG apps.

68
00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.757
Many people have worked
to create this course. I'd

69
00:03:57.757 --> 00:04:01.211
like to thank, on
the LlamaIndex side, Logan

70
00:04:01.211 --> 00:04:05.433
Markehwich, and
on the TruEra side, Shayak Sen, Joshua

71
00:04:05.433 --> 00:04:09.962
Reini, and Barbara Lewis. From
DeepLearning.ai, Eddie Shyu

72
00:04:09.962 --> 00:04:12.802
and Dialla Ezzeddine
also contributed to

73
00:04:12.802 --> 00:04:13.800
this course.

74
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.640
The next lesson will give
you an overview of what you'll

75
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.596
see in the rest of the course. You'll
try out question-answering systems that

76
00:04:20.596 --> 00:04:22.929
use sentence window
retrieval or auto-merging

77
00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:25.109
retrieval and compare
their performance on

78
00:04:25.109 --> 00:04:28.660
the RAG triad,
context relevance, groundedness, and answer relevance.

79
00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.690
Sounds great. Let's get started. And
I think you people

80
00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.840
are really clean up
with this RAG stuff.

81
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:35.920
Laugh on it.
